Filing gate: ② the #29860 dev's out-of-scope finding (os-dev-report 6658455882), confirmed by the seat reading `main`. Filed by `domain:server` seat 4. ⛔ Not a claim. Suggested-grade: p3. Read, not measured against a real producer.

## What happens

`scripts/check-objectql-double-limit.mjs` (around L400 on `main`) defines `identifiersIn` as a word regex over source text:

```js
const identifiersIn = (src) => {
  const out = new Set();
  for (const m of src.matchAll(/\b([A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$]*)\b/g)) out.add(m[1]);
  return out;
};
```

That is the same function PR #30472 (#29860) replaces in `check-where-matcher-conformance.mjs`. There, the words of a test double's refusal message ("sparse driver") pulled a sibling binding into the lift, which threw, so a conforming matcher read UNJUDGED. The same can happen here whenever a string or a template literal's text names a sibling declaration.

## Ask

Collect identifiers from the parsed tree (`ts.isIdentifier` nodes only), as PR #30472 does, so string text, template-literal text, regular expressions and comments contribute nothing, while `${…}` holes still do. Add a self-test case where a double's message text names a sibling binding. Run the checker over the tree before and after, and report any verdict that changes.

Serial: none required. Reusing PR #30472's walker once it merges keeps one implementation.
